        <section id="surface">
          <h2>Surface cleaning tells only part of the story</h2>

          <p>
            During everyday kitchen cleanup, staff may wipe the visible
            outside of the hood to remove splashes and fingerprints.
            That improves its appearance, but it does not establish the
            condition of the areas behind the filters or farther inside
            the exhaust system.
          </p>

          <p>
            When arranging restaurant hood cleaning, distinguish between
            cleaning exposed surfaces and addressing internal grease.
            Ask your provider to explain the proposed work in terms of
            actual components and accessible areas.
          </p>

          <div class="cards">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>What you can see</h3>
              <p>
                The exposed hood exterior may look bright and clean
                after routine wiping.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
              <h3>What still needs assessment</h3>
              <p>
                The hood interior, filter area and accessible exhaust
                passages may require a closer professional assessment.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="hidden-areas">
          <h2>Where grease can remain inside a restaurant hood</h2>

          <p>
            Every kitchen has a different equipment layout. These are
            useful areas to discuss when defining the scope of your
            commercial kitchen exhaust cleaning service.
          </p>

          <div class="cards">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Behind the hood filters</h3>
              <p>
                The space behind the filters is not fully visible
                during a quick look at the front of the hood.
                Ask whether this area is included in the assessment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
              <h3>Filter surfaces and edges</h3>
              <p>
                Discuss the condition of the filters and surrounding
                frames. Confirm whether filter cleaning is included
                in your service or quoted separately.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
              <h3>Interior corners and collection areas</h3>
              <p>
                Interior surfaces, corners and grease collection
                areas deserve attention beyond the appearance of
                the outer stainless steel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
              <h3>The exhaust entry and accessible ducts</h3>
              <p>
                Ask how the provider will assess the accessible
                passages beyond the hood and identify any areas
                that cannot be reached.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="note">
            <p>
              <strong>Access matters.</strong>
              Do not assume that every section can be reached through
              the hood opening. The cleaning company should explain
              access limitations and any further assessment needed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="signs">
          <h2>Changes worth reporting before your next cleaning</h2>

          <p>
            You do not need to diagnose the system yourself.
            Tell your service provider what you have noticed
            and when it started.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Visible grease or sticky residue around the hood</li>
            <li>Grease dripping from the hood or filter area</li>
            <li>A noticeable change in how cooking smoke is captured</li>
            <li>Cooking odors lingering longer than usual</li>
            <li>Uncertainty about when the system was last professionally cleaned</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Changes in ventilation can have several causes. A cleaning
            assessment can help establish the condition of accessible
            surfaces, but cleaning should not be treated as a guaranteed
            solution to every airflow or equipment problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Share these observations when requesting
            <a href="./#services">NYC hood cleaning services</a>
            so the provider can plan an appropriate assessment.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="scope">
          <h2>Ask what the hood cleaning service actually includes</h2>

          <p>
            A service name alone does not explain its full scope.
            Before booking, ask for a clear description of the components
            to be cleaned and any limitations.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Which hood surfaces are included?</strong>
              Ask about the interior as well as the exterior.
            </li>

            <li>
              <strong>Are the filters included?</strong>
              Confirm the filter quantity and whether cleaning them
              is part of the quoted work.
            </li>

            <li>
              <strong>What duct areas can be accessed?</strong>
              Discuss available openings and sections requiring
              further assessment.
            </li>

            <li>
              <strong>What is outside the quoted scope?</strong>
              Clarify additional equipment or separately priced work.
            </li>

            <li>
              <strong>How will the completed work be documented?</strong>
              Ask what records or photos will be available and how
              any uncleaned areas will be reported.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <div class="note">
            <p>
              <strong>A simple question that helps:</strong>
              “Can you show me which parts of my hood and exhaust
              system this quote covers?”
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="preparation">
          <h2>Prepare a few details for a more useful assessment</h2>

          <p>
            Clear equipment information helps the cleaning company
            understand your kitchen before the appointment.
            When contacting Best Commercial Hood Cleaning, include:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Your restaurant address and contact information</li>
            <li>The approximate hood length and number of filters</li>
            <li>The last cleaning date, if known</li>
            <li>Photos taken from a safe, readily accessible position</li>
            <li>Any visible buildup or recent changes you have noticed</li>
            <li>Your operating hours and preferred service time</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Do not open equipment or reach into inaccessible areas
            just to obtain photos. If the available pictures do not
            show enough detail, an on-site assessment may be needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can also review our
            <a href="blog.html#cleaning-quote">hood cleaning quote checklist</a>
            before sending your information.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Keep the focus on the agreed cleaning work</h2>

          <p>
            After service, review which components were cleaned
            and whether any access limitations remain. Keep the
            service documentation with your kitchen maintenance
            records.
          </p>

          <p>
            Photos can help explain the work completed, but a photo
            of one clean surface does not show the condition of
            every hidden section. Ask questions whenever the scope
            or documentation is unclear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Best Commercial Hood Cleaning works with restaurants,
            takeout kitchens, delis, pizzerias and other commercial
            kitchens across
            <a href="./#areas">NYC’s five boroughs</a>.
            Contact us to discuss your hood, filter and accessible
            exhaust cleaning needs.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="questions">
          <h2>Common questions about hidden hood grease</h2>

          <details>
            <summary>
              Does a shiny hood mean the exhaust system is clean?
            </summary>

            <p>
              No. The exterior appearance does not establish the
              condition behind the filters or inside the exhaust
              passages. Review the actual inspection and cleaning scope.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>
              Is cleaning the filters the same as cleaning the hood?
            </summary>

            <p>
              No. Filters are one component. Hood interiors and
              accessible exhaust passages are separate areas to
              discuss when arranging service.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>
              Can you provide a quote from photos?
            </summary>

            <p>
              Photos and equipment details can help with an initial
              discussion. The condition, layout or access requirements
              may make an on-site assessment necessary before the
              scope and price can be confirmed.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>
              Will hood cleaning fix poor ventilation?
            </summary>

            <p>
              It depends on the cause. Report any change in ventilation
              when requesting service. Equipment, airflow and cleaning
              issues may require different assessments or work.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

